both 100 and 150?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we start by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 100 and 150:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2d2354c0c449ab7d1d837793146147a492f702c8a412f724d5c9df2dad98a377"},"seconds":7.1794960498809814,"seed":23294575141909812,"stop":"eos","text":"Now, we know that any number that is a factor of both 100 and 150 must be a factor of their GCD, which is 50. The factors of 50 are:\n\\[\n1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\n\\]\n\nAmong these factors, the only two-digit factor is 50 itself. Therefore, there is only one positive two-digit integer that is a factor of both 100 and 150.\n\n\\boxed{1}"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"Now, we know that any number that is a factor of both 100 and 150 must be a factor of their GCD, which is 50. The factors of 50 are:\n\\[\n1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\n\\]\n\nAmong these factors, the only two-digit factor is 50 itself. Therefore, there is only one positive two-digit integer that is a factor of both 100 and 150.\n\n\\boxed{1}","id":"prealgebra:567"}
